WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.780
A child inherits one broken copy of a gene called RB1.

2
00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:13.080
That broken copy sits in every cell of the child.

3
00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:16.280
So why is the whole eye not a tumor?

4
00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:24.140
Most such children develop only a few tumors, mostly in the retina, and often in both eyes.

5
00:00:24.550 --> 00:00:28.830
The inherited mutation is only the first of two steps.

6
00:00:29.430 --> 00:00:35.270
A cell has two copies of the gene, and one working copy usually gives enough brake

7
00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:40.610
on growth. So a tumor needs a second hit that removes the working copy.

8
00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:42.850
That second hit is rare.

9
00:00:43.430 --> 00:00:47.710
It strikes only a few cells, and only those cells become tumor.

10
00:00:48.533 --> 00:00:50.893
Now count the second hits.

11
00:00:51.753 --> 00:00:55.193
Assume each tumor comes from an independent second hit.

12
00:00:56.053 --> 00:00:59.513
Say a carrier averages one and a half tumors per eye.

13
00:01:00.373 --> 00:01:03.193
The number of tumors then follows a Poisson curve.

14
00:01:03.953 --> 00:01:07.573
The chance of no tumor in one eye is 0.223.

15
00:01:08.753 --> 00:01:11.753
The chance of at least one is 0.777.

16
00:01:13.083 --> 00:01:17.463
Take both eyes together, and assume the eyes are independent.

17
00:01:18.583 --> 00:01:21.543
Tumors in both eyes: about 60%.

18
00:01:21.903 --> 00:01:25.903
A tumor in one eye only: about 35%.

19
00:01:25.903 --> 00:01:29.763
No tumor in either eye: about 5%.

20
00:01:29.763 --> 00:01:33.843
So about 95% of carriers get a tumor.

21
00:01:34.483 --> 00:01:36.523
That fraction is called penetrance.

22
00:01:37.567 --> 00:01:40.667
Now a child with no inherited copy.

23
00:01:41.387 --> 00:01:44.387
Both rare hits must fall in the same cell.

24
00:01:45.327 --> 00:01:50.147
Suppose the expected number of tumors per eye were 0.0001.

25
00:01:51.407 --> 00:01:56.287
A tumor in each eye would then have a chance of about one in a hundred million.

26
00:01:56.927 --> 00:01:59.707
So these tumors are single and one-sided.

27
00:02:00.487 --> 00:02:05.827
They also tend to be found later, because two events must pile up in one cell line.

28
00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:08.320
This resolves the puzzle.

29
00:02:08.980 --> 00:02:12.140
In a family, the inherited allele looks dominant.

30
00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:16.980
About half of a carrier's children inherit it, and most of them develop tumors.

31
00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:21.780
Yet inside one cell, the same allele is recessive.

32
00:02:22.340 --> 00:02:23.960
A pedigree follows risk.

33
00:02:24.560 --> 00:02:26.080
A cell counts copies.

34
00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:32.020
So an inherited first hit is dominant in the family and recessive in the cell.
